.TH BIFOUT 1 "October 2026" "BIDeT 4D" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bifout \- draw a BIF (BIDeT Intermediate Format) as a PNG, GIF, SIXEL, kitty, iTerm2, coloured text or Tektronix picture, or play it on a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bifout
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIFILE.bif\fR]
.br
.B bifin
\&... |
.B bifop
\&... |
.B bifout
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bifout
is the third tool of BIDeT 4D.  It reads a BIF, from a file or from standard input, and draws it: as a PNG, as an
animated GIF, or on a terminal in whatever that terminal can show (SIXEL, the kitty or iTerm2 image protocols, coloured text,
Tektronix vectors; see FORMATS).  Line art is drawn from vectors, so it is as sharp at
.B \-\-scale 6
as at
.BR "\-\-scale 1" .
A BIF with several frames is an animation: it is written as a GIF, or, if it is going to a terminal as SIXEL or
as coloured text, played there.
.PP
The format is chosen from the name given to
.BR \-o :
.I .png
.IR ".gif" ,
.I .six
or
.IR .sixel ,
.I .ans
(or
.IR .ansi )
or
.IR .tek ;
with no file name it is
.B auto
if standard output is a terminal (SIXEL if the terminal has it), else PNG.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR, " \-\-output " FILE"
Write here (\fB\-\fR is standard output).
.TP
.BI \-f " FORMAT" "\fR, " \-\-format " FORMAT" "\fR, " \-s "\fR, " \-\-sixel
Force the format: \fBpng\fR, \fBgif\fR, \fBsixel\fR, \fBkitty\fR, \fBiterm\fR, \fBansi\fR, \fBtek\fR, \fBtek\-hatch\fR,
\fBtek\-dots\fR, \fBtek\-contour\fR, or \fBauto\fR (for a terminal only); \fB\-s\fR is \fB\-f sixel\fR.  See FORMATS.
.TP
.BI \-\-cols " N" "\fR, " \-\-rows " N"
The size in character cells.  \fBkitty\fR and \fBiterm\fR: scale the picture to this many cells.  \fBansi\fR: the width and height
of the text (negative: that many fewer than the terminal; default: the picture's own size at 8 pixels a character, at most the
terminal's width).  The picture's shape is kept.
.TP
.BI \-\-glyphs " half" | quad | blocks | braille
\fBansi\fR: the characters to use: half blocks; plus quadrants; plus eighths (the default); Braille dots.
.TP
.BI \-\-colors " 24" | 256 | 16
\fBansi\fR: the colour depth: 24-bit (the default), 256 or 16 colours.
.TP
.B \-\-tek\-invert
\fBtek\-hatch\fR, \fBtek\-dots\fR, \fBtek\-contour\fR: dark is ink (for a picture of dark shading on a light page).
.TP
.BI \-\-scale " S" "\fR, " \-\-width " PX\fR, " \-\-height " PX"
Draw at S pixels per canvas unit, or fit the picture into this size.
.TP
.BI \-\-ink " COLOR" "\fR, " \-\-paper " COLOR\fR, " \-b " auto|transparent|COLOR"
Replace the ink colour; the page colour; or choose the page for a terminal:
.B auto
asks the terminal for its background (OSC 11) and draws on that (SIXEL), or leaves the page transparent (kitty, iterm, ansi,
or if the terminal will not say), and makes the ink light or dark so that it shows.
.TP
.B \-\-frame \fIN\fR
Draw only this frame of an animation (a still).  Without it a GIF gets all the frames, and a terminal plays them.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " F" "\fR, " \-\-fps " N\fR, " \-\-loop " N"
Animation: play F times as fast; at most N frames a second (fewer is quicker to draw and smaller); N times
(0 = until interrupted).
.TP
.BI \-\-cell " WxH"
Animation on a terminal: the size of a character cell in pixels, if the terminal does not say (default: asked, then the
window size, then 10x20).
.TP
.BR \-\-erase " " ech | bg | none
Animation on a terminal whose colour is not known (transparent frames): how an earlier frame is kept from showing through.
.B ech
(the default) erases the picture's character cells first;
.B bg
has the terminal fill unpainted pixels with its own background (SIXEL P2=0);
.B none
does neither.  See TERMINALS.
.TP
.B \-\-transparent
Leave the page transparent (a GIF with a clear colour, PNG alpha, SIXEL unpainted).
.TP
.B \-\-force\fR, \fB\-\-no\-query
Send SIXEL even if the terminal does not say that it can; do not ask the terminal anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-background " auto|transparent|COLOR"
The long form of
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-\-ss " N" "\fR, " \-\-layer " ID\fR, " \-\-max\-pixels " N"
The supersampling of vector layers (default: automatic, up to 4); draw only this layer id (repeat for several); the largest
picture to draw, beyond which it is drawn smaller.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-truncated
Draw what arrived of a file that ends early.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Say what was drawn, and how big; print the version.
.PP
Everything else is in
.BR "bifout \-\-help" .
.SH FORMATS
.TP
.B png, gif
Files.  A GIF holds all the frames of an animation.
.TP
.B sixel
SIXEL graphics, the format most terminals that draw pictures share.  A terminal is asked whether it has it, and one that says
it has not gets an error, not garbage (\fB\-\-force\fR to send it anyway).  Plays an animation.
.TP
.B kitty
The kitty graphics protocol (APC), for kitty, WezTerm, Ghostty and some others: the picture as a PNG in chunks of 4096 bytes, with
its transparency.  One frame.
.TP
.B iterm
iTerm2's inline images (OSC 1337), for iTerm2, WezTerm, mintty and some others: the picture as a PNG in one sequence, with its
transparency.  One frame.
.TP
.B ansi
Coloured Unicode text, which any UTF-8 terminal shows: every character cell is an 8 by 8 patch of the picture, and the block,
quadrant, eighth or Braille character and the two colours that fit it best are chosen (\fB\-\-glyphs\fR, \fB\-\-colors\fR).  A
transparent page leaves the terminal's own background showing.  A file of it can be shown with \fBcat\fR.  Plays an animation.
.TP
.B tek
Tektronix 4010/4014 vector graphics, for xterm's Tek mode (\fBxterm \-t\fR) and real Tektronix terminals: the picture's own paths,
as strokes (a letter as its outline; no colour, no thickness).  A picture with no vector layer is drawn as
.BR tek\-hatch .
On an xterm the terminal is put into Tek mode, the picture drawn, Enter waited for, and the VT window restored; into a file or
a pipe it is a plain Tek stream.  Everything is drawn at 1024 by 780, the 4010's addressing.
.TP
.B tek\-hatch, tek\-dots, tek\-contour
Tektronix vectors made from the picture's pixels, bright meaning ink: horizontal scan lines, denser where it is brighter; dots,
Floyd-Steinberg dithered; or outlines and brightness contour lines, like a map.
.TP
.B auto
(on a terminal, the default) whichever the terminal can show: \fBkitty\fR or \fBiterm\fR if the environment says that is the
terminal, else \fBsixel\fR if the terminal reports it (or nothing could be asked), else \fBansi\fR.  The formats not listed
here as playing an animation draw its first frame, and say so.
.SH ANIMATION
On a terminal that is sent SIXEL (or coloured text, with \fB\-f ansi\fR, which works on any UTF-8 terminal), an animation is
drawn and encoded once, SIXEL on one shared palette of 254 colours, and then replayed on a
steady clock until interrupted with Ctrl-C.  A terminal that cannot keep up skips frames; it does not slow the motion down.
A GIF is written with timings rounded from the running total to its hundredths of a second.
Into a pipe, a
.I .six
file or a PNG only frame 0 can go, and bifout says so.
.SH TERMINALS
SIXEL support differs from terminal to terminal, and animation puts it under the most strain.
.B bifout
has been tested thoroughly only in mintty, where it works well.
.B Your mileage may vary
(YMMV) in other terminals.
.PP
In Windows Terminal, when its background colour is not known, animations show noise and leftover pixels of earlier frames.
Soft shadows are not drawn there, since the page is unknown.
.B \-\-erase bg
was tried and is worse, not better.  Other terminals (WezTerm, foot, xterm \-ti vt340, mlterm, iTerm2 ...) have not been tried
by the author.  Until a terminal's own SIXEL handling improves, the dependable way to see an animation is to write it as a
GIF with
.BR "\-o FILE.gif" .
.PP
Cygwin's own console (the tty is /dev/cons0: a Windows Terminal tab that runs bash directly) swallows the OSC 11 query for the
background colour, although Windows Terminal itself answers it.  There
.B bifout
asks only whether SIXEL is available (DA1), assumes a dark page, and prints a note once on stderr (the marker file is
.I cygwin\-console\-warned
in
.I $BIDET_CACHE
or
.IR ~/.cache/bidet ).
Ways round it: mintty (a pty); a colour given with
.BR "\-b #RRGGBB" ;
or, clunky but tested with Windows Terminal 1.24 and Cygwin 3.6, a PowerShell tab that logs in to the machine with Windows' own
OpenSSH client
.RB ( "C:\eWindows\eSystem32\eOpenSSH\essh.exe MACHINE" ),
which needs the Cygwin sshd running there: the shell is then a pty, the query is answered and animations play without the noise.
It also works as the command line of a Windows Terminal profile.  Tested on two machines.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BIDET_NO_QUERY
If set, never ask the terminal anything.
.TP
.B BIDET_CELL
The character cell, as WxH in pixels, for an animation (see \fB\-\-cell\fR).
.TP
.B LSIX_FORCE_SIXEL_SUPPORT
If set, send SIXEL even if the terminal does not say it can.
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
Read, when the terminal will not say its colours, to guess whether it is light or dark.
.TP
.BR KITTY_WINDOW_ID ", " TERM ", " TERM_PROGRAM ", " LC_TERMINAL
Read by \fB\-f auto\fR to tell kitty (also Ghostty: \fBTERM_PROGRAM=ghostty\fR) and iTerm2 from the others.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bidet (1),
.BR bifin (1),
.BR bifop (1).
The format is described in BIF\-SPEC.md, and the whole design in README.md, in the share/doc/bidet4d directory;
GRAPHICS\-HISTORY.md there says why a picture can reach a terminal in so many different ways.
